          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (stabilization for
                = succeed "-categories)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,1)
            %p
              Equipping an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2C1%29-category"} (n,1)-category
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              compatible monoidal structures for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≥
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \geq n + 2
              (which is the same as equipping it with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+k-cubes+operad"} little k-cubes operad
              action) is the same as equipping it with the structure of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (n,1)-category
          %p
            An aspect of this was demonstrated in terms of
            %span.newWikiWord
              Tamsamani n-categories
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Tamsamani+n-categories"} ?
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
              ,
              %em
                On the Breen-Baez-Dolan stabilization hypothesis for Tamsamani’s weak
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9810058"}> arXiv:math/9810058
              )
          %p
            A proof of the full statement in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
            is sketched in
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/IMA/Joyal.pdf#page=187"} section 43.5
            of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              ,
              %em Notes on quasi-categories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/IMA/Joyal.pdf"}> pdf
              ).
          %p Probably the first full proof in print is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ek-Algebras"} Ek-Algebras
              ,
          %p where it appears in example 1.2.3 as a direct consequence of a more general statement, corollary 1.1.10.
          %h3#other_lowdimensional_cases Other low-dimensional cases
          %p One expects that
          %ul
            %li pointed 2-connected tricategories can be identified with commutative monoids (again),
            %li pointed 1-connected tricategories can be identified with braided monoidal categories,
            %li pointed 0-connected tricategories can be identified with monoidal bicategories,
            %li and so on.
          %h2#references References
          %p Some discussion of the peridodic table is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenia+Cheng"}> Eugenia Cheng
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Gurski"} Nick Gurski
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1178"} arXiv:0708.1178
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0608420"} arXiv:math.CT/0608420
          %p
            The theory of
            = succeed "-tuply" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            was maybe first studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %strong EnAction
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Francis"} John Francis
              PhD thesis (
              %a{:href => "http://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/43792"}> web
              )
          %p and later further developed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ek-Algebras"} Ek-Algebras
          %p
            where also the proof of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization+hypothesis"} stabilization hypothesis
            in this context is noticed.
          %p
            Related discussion can be found in the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iterated+monoidal+category"} iterated monoidal categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                C. Balteanu, Z. Fiedorowicz, R. Schwänzl,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rainer+Vogt"} Rainer Vogt
                %em Iterated monoidal categories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.45.290&rep=rep1&type=pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clemens+Berger"}> Clemens Berger
                ,
                %em Double loop spaces, braided monoidal categories and algebraic 3-type of space
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.unice.fr/~cberger/bmonoid.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %h2#discussion Discussion
          %p A previous version of this entry led to the following discussion
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: I would like to suggest that we switch to using
              %em symmetric monoidal
              rather than
              = succeed "," do
                %em stably monoidal
              = succeed "." do
                %em stable
              = succeed "-category\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-category.\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %p
              Use of the word “stable” here also creates confusion with its other meanings (see
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/01/benzvi_on_geometric_function_t.html#c021217"} here
              and
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/01/categories_logic_and_physics_i_2.html#c021205"} here
              = succeed ",\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
              = succeed "-groupoids." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %i Connective
              spectra can be identified with symmetric
              %em groupal
              = succeed "-groupoids.)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed "-category\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %p
              %em> Toby
              \: Hopefully John will admit that saying ‘stable’ instead of ‘stably monoidal’ (or ‘stably groupal’) was a slip of the tongue … pen … fingers. I'm used to ‘stably monoidal’, and I don't think that it should cause confusion —if used in full. Also, I think there's some historical confusion about ‘symmetric monoidal
              = succeed "-category\u2019" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              = succeed "-category\u2019" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
